<title>USA: U.S. August consumer prices rose 0.1 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. August consumer prices rose 0.1 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-09-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department</p>
<p>monthly Consumer Price Index (CPI-U), 1982-84 equals 100:</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Aug   July    Aug'96/95</p>
<p> All Items		    0.1    0.3	2.9</p>
<p> -Excluding Food/Energy   0.1    0.3	2.6</p>
<p> Energy			-0.6   -0.4	3.9</p>
<p> Food, Drink		  0.3    0.5	3.6</p>
<p> Food			   0.4    0.5	3.6</p>
<p> CPI-Urban Consumers-X  157.3  157.0	N/A</p>
<p>X-Data unadjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Aug   July    Aug'96/95</p>
<p> Services		     0.2    0.5	3.2</p>
<p> Housing			0.2    0.4	2.9</p>
<p> -Home Fuel Oil	     0.5   -1.6	6.5</p>
<p> -Homeowner Costs-Y	 0.2    0.3	3.1</p>
<p> Apparel, Upkeep	   -1.4   -0.1     -1.5</p>
<p> Transportation	    -0.2   -0.2	2.6</p>
<p> -Gasoline		   -2.0   -2.7	5.0</p>
<p> -New Cars		    0.3    0.2	2.0</p>
<p>Y-Dec 1982=100 base.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Aug   July    Aug'96/95</p>
<p> Medical Care		 0.2    0.3	3.4</p>
<p> Commodities		 -0.1    0.1	2.3</p>
<p> CPI-W			  0.1    0.2	2.9</p>
<p>CPI-W is the Consumer Price Index for urban wage earners and clerical workers.</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a 0.2 percent rise in August consumer prices. Excluding food and energy, they also expected a 0.2 percent increase.</p>
<p>In the first eight months of 1996, consumer prices rose at a 3.2 percent seasonally adjusted annual rate, compared with a 2.5 percent increase for all of 1995.</p>
<p>The department said the August increase in the CPI was the smallest monthly gain since a 0.1 percent rise in June. The CPI increase excluding food and energy was the smallest monthly rise since a 0.1 percent increase in April.</p>
<p>The apparel and upkeep component of the index in August posted its largest monthly drop since a 1.9 percent decline in January 1949.</p>
